<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fritigern</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 380</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fritigern was a leader of the Thervingi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> in the 370s. In 376, fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, his followers were admitted across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, but abuse by Roman officials drove them to revolt. He led the Gothic army that destroyed the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> and his army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">Battle of Adrianople</a> in 378.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fritigern was a Thervingian Gothic chieftain whose name is reconstructed as Frithugairns, desiring peace. The earliest references concern the years between an attack on the Thervingi by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> and the Hunnic raids of about 376. Socrates Scholasticus, Sozomen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a> report a civil war between Fritigern and Athanaric, another prominent Thervingian ruler; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> and Philostorgius do not mention it. According to Socrates, Fritigern asked for Roman aid and, with Valens, defeated Athanaric. Before or during this conflict he became an Arian Christian. Athanaric seems to have prevailed, since he later led the Thervingi against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 376 the Thervingi, pressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> who had already conquered their kinsmen the Greuthungi, were allowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In the winter of 376-377 famine struck them. The Roman governors Lupicinus and Maximus sold them food at extreme prices, so that many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> sold their children into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and invited several leaders to a feast where some were killed and others taken hostage.</p><p>Fritigern escaped, became leader of the Thervingi and declared war. His forces beat Lupicinus at the Battle of Marcianople, and by 377 they held most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Thracia</a>. He held a wagon fort through the winter and won Hunnic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanic-language">Alanic</a> cavalry to his side.</p><p>On the night of 8 August he sent envoys proposing peace and an alliance in exchange for territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, with a private letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>. Valens refused and marched. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">Battle of Adrianople</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> won a decisive victory, the Roman army was routed and Valens was killed. Fritigern besieged Adrianople without success. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> succeeded Valens in January 379, and peace was declared on 3 October 382.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fritigern” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 376 the Huns attacked, and a large group under <b>Fritigern</b> was admitted across the Danube.</blockquote><a href="/state/tervingi">Tervingi</a></li></ul></section>
